From: Tariq Bashir <tbashir@brightsign.biz>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 16:30:07 +0100
Subject: Brightsign: Do not initialize ICU datetime with serialized datetime
 name string OS-21133

Cherry pick of Chromium 87 change: I1435311d040541733865adc933f73bdb5f8ea289

Chromium reads the timezone from ICU in Olson timezone id form. Sends it
via IPC to render process. Render process reads it and initializes ICU
with the given string.

If a POSIX format doesn't exactly match the version in ICU, ICU cannot
provide a Olson timezone name, it gives the first part of POSIX string
as timezone name. When ICU is initialized with this string in render
process, ICU sets the timezone name as a invalid string. Which in the
end, causes an exception to be thrown when Int.DateTimeFormat() is
called.

This commit removes the ICU initialization with serialized string. ICU
gets initialized itself by TZ without explicit initialization. In the
end, it still cannot figure out the timezone name from POSIX date time
format, but it doesn't throw an exception when queried.

diff --git a/content/zygote/zygote_linux.cc b/content/zygote/zygote_linux.cc
index 3bbfaf0c6f24866ff262827d849b799a1f7c69c2..9cdb1c35c9a3f3c43ec8c6997122b99ce8ccb7f4 100644
--- a/content/zygote/zygote_linux.cc
+++ b/content/zygote/zygote_linux.cc
@@ -568,8 +568,6 @@ base::ProcessId Zygote::ReadArgsAndFork(base::PickleIterator iter,
   std::u16string timezone_id;
   if (!iter.ReadString16(&timezone_id))
     return -1;
-  icu::TimeZone::adoptDefault(icu::TimeZone::createTimeZone(
-      icu::UnicodeString(false, timezone_id.data(), timezone_id.length())));
 
   if (!iter.ReadUInt32(&uid))
     return -1;
